.TH TRACE_NAME 1 "2026-02-03" "TRACE" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
TRACE_NAME \- how TRACE/TLOG names are formed
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B TLOG
\- streaming trace macro
.BR TRACEN "(name, lvl, ...)" 
.SH DESCRIPTION
This manual page describes how the trace "name" is determined for entries produced by the TRACE/TLOG facility.

The name used to index per-name trace levels may come from several sources (precedence):
.TP
.B 1.
An explicit, non-empty name passed to the calling macro (for example, \fBTRACEN("myname", ...)\fR).
.TP
.B 2.
The \fBTRACE_NAME\fR specification (a macro defined in source before including the headers).
.TP
.B 3.
The \fBTRACE_NAME\fR environment variable (if set and non-empty).
.TP
.B 4.
The compile-time default \fBTRACE_DFLT_NAME\fR (currently "%f %H").

.SH FORMAT SPEC
The \fBTRACE_NAME\fR specification is a small format language parsed by \fBtrace_name_path()\fR. Common sequences:
.TP
.B %%
literal percent
.TP
.B %f / %h
filename (file/hdrf) without extension
.TP
.B %F / %H
filename (file/hdrf) with extension
.TP
.B %[-][0-9]f
include additional path components (0-9)
.TP
.B %p
program name (if enabled at compile-time)

If the spec contains a space, the two parts are used separately: the first for base/main files, the second for included/header files (this is why the default uses "%f %H").

.SH IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
.PP
When a non-empty explicit name is provided, it is passed verbatim to \fBtrace_name2TID()\fR. Otherwise \fBtrace_tlog_name_()\fR builds a name from the spec (macro/env/default) and then calls \fBtrace_name2TID()\fR.

Non-graph or non-printable characters in names are replaced with '_' before insertion; long names are truncated from the left so the trailing portion (usually the most meaningful) is preserved.

.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Default behavior: \fBTRACE_DFLT_NAME\fR is "%f %H" so main files use basename-without-extension and headers use filename-with-extension.

Set via environment:
.nf
export TRACE_NAME="%p-%-f"
.fi

.SH SHORT EXAMPLES
.PP
.TP
.B 1. Define-before-include (recommended):
.nf
/* Define module default TRACE_NAME, then include headers */
#define TRACE_NAME "%p-%2f %~src/%H"
#include "trace.h"

int main(void) {
	TLOG_INFO() << "uses programname-2pathcomponents"; /* expands using TRACE_NAME */
}
.fi
.PP
.TP
.B 2. Temporary define/undef around a block of uses:
.nf
#include "trace.h"

/* Temporarily override TRACE_NAME for the next block of TLOG/TRACEN uses */
#define TRACE_NAME "%p-%-f %H"
TLOG_INFO() << "temporary name used here";
TRACEN("explicit", TLVL_INFO, "explicit used verbatim");
#undef TRACE_NAME

/* Back to default / env-controlled behavior */
TLOG_INFO() << "default name used here";
.fi
.PP
.TP
.B 3. Explicit name passed to TRACEN (verbatim):
.nf
#include "trace.h"

TRACEN("my_module,subpart", TLVL_INFO, "message: %d", 42);
.fi

.SH ORDERING AND RECOMMENDATION
.PP
.B Macro expansion:  \fBTLOG\fR/\fBTRACEN\fR macros are expanded at the site of use, so a \fB#define TRACE_NAME\fR placed after \fB#include "trace.h"\fR still affects subsequent macro expansions in the same translation unit.
.PP
.B Fallback symbol:  If \fBTRACE_NAME\fR was not defined when the header was included, the header emits a fallback symbol `static const char *TRACE_NAME = NULL;`. Defining a preprocessor macro with the same name later is allowed but can be confusing — to avoid ambiguity and the fallback symbol, prefer defining \fBTRACE_NAME\fR before including \fBtrace.h\fR or control it via the environment.

.SH SEE ALSO
.BR include/trace.h

